      "A room-by-room breakdown of a standard clean and what's add-on vs. deep-clean territory.",
    contentHtml: `
<p>When you book a house cleaning, it helps to know exactly what your cleaning team will focus on — and what falls outside the scope of a typical visit. A <a href="/services/standard-cleaning">standard cleaning</a> is built to keep your home consistently fresh between deeper sessions, covering the surfaces, floors, and fixtures that see daily use. Here's a room-by-room look at what's included, so there are no surprises on cleaning day.</p>

<h2>Kitchen</h2>
<p>The kitchen usually gets the most attention in a standard visit since it sees the heaviest daily wear. Expect your cleaning team to wipe down countertops, backsplashes, and the exterior of appliances like the refrigerator, oven, microwave, and dishwasher. Sinks are scrubbed and disinfected, stovetops are cleaned of everyday grease and food splatter, and the outside of cabinets is wiped down to remove fingerprints and smudges. Floors are swept and mopped, trash is emptied and re-lined, and any dishes left in the sink are typically washed or loaded into the dishwasher as a courtesy, depending on your plan.</p>

<h2>Bathrooms</h2>
<p>Bathrooms get a full sanitizing pass. This includes scrubbing and disinfecting toilets, sinks, tubs, and showers, wiping down mirrors and countertops, and polishing chrome fixtures. Floors are swept and mopped, trash is emptied, and towels are typically straightened or replaced if you leave clean ones out. The goal is a bathroom that looks and feels sanitized after every visit, not just tidied up.</p>

<h2>Bedrooms and Living Areas</h2>
<p>In bedrooms, living rooms, and other common spaces, a standard clean covers dusting of reachable surfaces, including dressers, nightstands, shelves, and tabletops. Beds are typically made (sheets changed if fresh linens are left out), mirrors and glass surfaces are wiped, and floors are vacuumed or mopped depending on flooring type. Light general tidying, such as fluffing pillows or straightening throw blankets, is often included, though organizing closets or personal belongings usually is not.</p>

<h2>Whole-Home Tasks</h2>
<p>Beyond individual rooms, a standard cleaning covers tasks that apply throughout the home. This includes vacuuming carpets and rugs, mopping hard floors, dusting reachable surfaces and baseboards, wiping down light switches and door handles, and emptying trash bins in every room. Staircases are typically vacuumed or swept, and entryways are given a quick once-over. These whole-home tasks are what make a <a href="/services/recurring-cleaning">recurring cleaning</a> schedule so effective — the same core checklist is completed consistently, so buildup never has a chance to accumulate between visits.</p>

<h2>What's Not Included: Standard Clean vs. Deep Clean</h2>
<p>A standard cleaning is designed for upkeep, not restoration. That means certain tasks fall outside the typical visit and are considered <a href="/services/deep-cleaning">deep cleaning</a> territory instead. These usually include:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Interior oven and refrigerator cleaning</li>
  <li>Washing interior windows and window tracks</li>
  <li>Scrubbing baseboards, vents, and light fixtures in detail</li>
  <li>Cleaning inside kitchen cabinets and drawers</li>
  <li>Descaling built-up grime or hard water stains in showers</li>
  <li>Moving furniture or appliances to clean behind and underneath them</li>
  <li>Wall spot-cleaning or scrubbing scuff marks</li>
</ul>
<p>If your home hasn't had a professional cleaning in a while, or if you're moving in or out of a space, a deep cleaning is usually the better starting point. From there, a standard cleaning on a recurring basis keeps everything at that reset level so buildup never returns.</p>

<h3>Customizing Your Cleaning</h3>
<p>Every home is different, and most cleaning plans can be adjusted to fit your priorities. If there's a specific area you want extra attention on, or a task you'd like added to your regular visit, it's worth mentioning when you book so your cleaning team knows what matters most to you.</p>

<h2>Choosing the Right Plan for Your Home</h2>
<p>Understanding what's included in a standard clean makes it easier to decide how often you need service and whether a deep clean should come first. Many homeowners start with a deep clean to reset the space, then move to a standard cleaning on a recurring schedule to maintain it. Others prefer standard visits from the start and simply add deep-clean tasks periodically as needed. Either way, knowing the boundaries between the two helps you get the most value out of every visit.</p>

<p>Ready to see what a standard clean would do for your home? <a href="/contact">Request a free quote from Spottly</a> and pick a time that works for you.</p>
`,
