    contentHtml: `
<h2>What a standard clean covers</h2>
<p>A standard clean is the workhorse of home maintenance. It's designed to keep your living space consistently tidy and sanitary between visits, addressing the surfaces and areas that accumulate everyday dust, grime, and clutter. Think of it as the reset your home needs on a regular basis rather than a deep restoration project.</p>
<p>During a <a href="/services/standard-cleaning">standard cleaning</a> visit, you can expect the team to wipe down countertops, dust furniture and accessible surfaces, vacuum carpets and rugs, mop hard floors, clean bathroom fixtures, and empty trash bins. Kitchens get a wipe-down of exterior surfaces, and bathrooms are sanitized where it matters most — sinks, toilets, and showers. It's efficient, thorough for its scope, and ideal for homes that are already in reasonably good shape.</p>
<p>This is the service most homeowners rely on week to week or month to month. It doesn't involve moving furniture, scrubbing inside appliances, or tackling buildup that's settled into grout lines or baseboards over months. Instead, it's about maintaining a baseline of cleanliness so your home never drifts too far from where you want it.</p>

<h2>What a deep clean covers</h2>
<p>A deep clean goes several layers further. It's built for the spaces and details that a standard visit doesn't reach — the buildup that accumulates slowly and eventually needs dedicated attention. A <a href="/services/deep-cleaning">deep cleaning</a> service typically includes scrubbing baseboards, cleaning inside the oven and refrigerator, washing interior windows, detailing grout and tile, wiping down cabinet fronts and interiors, dusting ceiling fans and light fixtures, and addressing areas behind and underneath furniture and appliances.</p>
<p>Every inch gets more attention. Bathrooms are scrubbed more intensively, including tile, fixtures, and often areas like exhaust fans or vents. Kitchens get the same treatment, with appliances, cabinet exteriors, and hard-to-reach corners all part of the scope. Because it involves more time, more product, and more physical effort, a deep clean is naturally a bigger undertaking than a standard visit.</p>
<p>Deep cleaning is often recommended as a starting point for new clients, since it establishes a true baseline before regular maintenance begins. It's also the right call after long stretches without professional cleaning, or when a home simply needs a reset.</p>

<h3>Common deep-clean triggers</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Moving into or out of a home</li>
  <li>Preparing for guests, a holiday, or a special event</li>
  <li>Returning from an extended vacation or work trip</li>
  <li>Seasonal refreshes, such as a spring or year-end clean</li>
  <li>The first booking with a new cleaning service</li>
</ul>

<h2>Signs you need a deep clean first</h2>
<p>If it's been several months since your home has had professional attention, a standard clean may not be enough to get you where you want to be. Here are some signs a deep clean should come first:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Visible buildup in the kitchen</strong> — grease on cabinet fronts, grime inside the oven, or sticky residue on backsplashes.</li>
  <li><strong>Grout and tile discoloration</strong> — dark lines in bathroom or kitchen grout that a quick wipe won't lift.</li>
  <li><strong>Dust accumulation on high surfaces</strong> — ceiling fans, vents, and light fixtures that haven't been touched in a while.</li>
  <li><strong>Baseboards and corners that look untouched</strong> — dust and debris that's settled along edges and behind furniture.</li>
  <li><strong>A general sense of staleness</strong> — the home feels cluttered or heavy even after a light tidy-up.</li>
</ul>
<p>Skipping straight to a standard clean when these signs are present usually leads to disappointment, since the service isn't designed to tackle that level of buildup. Starting with a deep clean sets a proper foundation.</p>

<h2>How to decide</h2>
<p>The simplest way to choose is to think about timing and condition. If your home has been professionally cleaned recently and you're just maintaining it, a standard clean is the right fit. If it's been a while, if you're moving, or if you're noticing the signs above, book a deep clean first.</p>
<p>Many homeowners find the most effective approach is pairing the two: a deep clean to establish a clean baseline, followed by <a href="/services/recurring-cleaning">recurring cleaning</a> on a weekly, biweekly, or monthly schedule to maintain it. This combination tends to be more cost-effective over time than repeated deep cleans, since regular maintenance prevents the kind of heavy buildup that requires intensive work in the first place.</p>
<p>If you're still unsure which service fits your home's current condition, it often helps to describe your space and cleaning history so a professional can recommend the right starting point.</p>

<p>Not sure which one your home needs? <a href="/contact">Request a free quote from Spottly</a> and we'll help you pick.</p>
`,
