      "What landlords inspect and how a move-out clean protects your deposit under CA's one-month cap.",
    contentHtml: `
<p>Moving out of a rental is stressful enough without wondering whether you'll get your full security deposit back. In Los Angeles, where deposits often represent a significant chunk of your moving budget, the condition of the unit at your final walkthrough can make the difference between a full refund and a set of deductions you didn't see coming. A thorough move-out clean is one of the most effective ways to protect that money, but it only works if you know what landlords are actually looking for.</p>

<h2>What landlords actually inspect</h2>
<p>Most landlords and property managers use a standard move-in condition report as their baseline, comparing it against the unit's condition when you leave. The goal is to distinguish normal wear and tear, which they cannot legally deduct for, from actual damage or lack of cleanliness, which they can.</p>
<p>During a move-out inspection, expect attention to detail in a few key areas:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Kitchen surfaces and appliances</strong> — grease buildup on stovetops, inside ovens, and range hoods; grime inside the refrigerator; crumbs and residue in cabinets and drawers.</li>
  <li><strong>Bathrooms</strong> — soap scum on tile and glass, mineral buildup around faucets, and buildup around toilet bases.</li>
  <li><strong>Floors</strong> — stains or embedded dirt in carpet, scuff marks on hard flooring, and dust along baseboards.</li>
  <li><strong>Walls and fixtures</strong> — scuffs, fingerprints around light switches, and dust on ceiling fans or vents.</li>
  <li><strong>Windows and tracks</strong> — dirt and debris trapped in window tracks and sliding door channels.</li>
  <li><strong>Overall odor and general upkeep</strong> — lingering smells from cooking, pets, or smoking are frequently noted on inspection sheets.</li>
</ul>
<p>A landlord's job at this stage is essentially to document any gap between how the unit was received and how it's being returned. A clean, well-maintained space narrows that gap and gives them far less to point to.</p>

<h2>The cleaning checklist that protects your deposit</h2>
<p>A move-out clean should go well beyond a typical weekly tidy-up. Because the goal is to return the unit as close as possible to its original condition, it helps to work room by room with an inspection mindset rather than a "good enough" one.</p>
<h3>Kitchen</h3>
<p>Degrease the stovetop and oven interior, wipe down cabinet fronts and interiors, clean the refrigerator inside and out, and disinfect countertops and the sink. Don't forget the exterior of appliances, including the tops of the fridge and any built-in microwave.</p>
<h3>Bathrooms</h3>
<p>Scrub tile, grout, and glass shower doors, clean and disinfect the toilet inside and out, wipe down vanities and mirrors, and address any mineral deposits around fixtures.</p>
<h3>Floors and baseboards</h3>
<p>Vacuum and, if needed, shampoo carpets to lift embedded dirt and odors. Mop hard flooring thoroughly, and wipe down baseboards where dust tends to accumulate unnoticed.</p>
<h3>Walls, doors, and fixtures</h3>
<p>Spot-clean marks on walls and doors, wipe light switches and door handles, dust ceiling fans, light fixtures, and vents, and clean window sills and tracks.</p>
<h3>Closets and storage areas</h3>
<p>Empty and wipe down closet shelving, vacuum closet floors, and check cabinets and drawers throughout the unit for anything left behind.</p>
<p>If a full deep clean feels like more than you can manage during a hectic move, a professional <a href="/services/deep-cleaning">deep cleaning</a> service can cover these details in one visit, which is often more efficient than trying to fit it in around packing and logistics.</p>

<h2>California's security deposit cap: what changed</h2>
<p>Security deposits in California are governed by state law, and that law changed in 2024. Under California AB 12, effective July 1, 2024, most residential security deposits are now capped at one month's rent, a reduction from the previous limits that allowed landlords to collect more depending on whether a unit was furnished.</p>
<p>This cap matters for renters for a simple reason: with less deposit money on the table, landlords and property managers have less room to absorb the cost of cleaning or repairs themselves, and more incentive to scrutinize the unit's condition closely at move-out. A smaller deposit doesn't mean a smaller stake for tenants — if anything, it raises the importance of returning the unit in clean, well-maintained condition, since there's less cushion to cover any deductions.</p>

<h2>Documenting your move-out</h2>
<p>Even after a thorough clean, documentation is your best protection if a dispute arises. Before handing over your keys, walk through the unit with your phone and photograph or video every room, paying particular attention to areas landlords commonly flag: kitchen surfaces, bathroom fixtures, flooring, and walls.</p>
<p>A few habits make this documentation more useful:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Time-stamp your photos or videos, or send them to yourself via email or text right away so there's a clear record of when they were taken.</li>
  <li>Capture wide shots of each room as well as close-ups of any areas that were previously noted as damaged on your move-in checklist, showing they've been addressed or were never an issue.</li>
  <li>Keep a copy of your original move-in condition report alongside your move-out photos for direct comparison.</li>
  <li>Note the date and time you returned keys, and to whom, in writing.</li>
</ul>
<p>This kind of documentation doesn't just help if a landlord tries to make an improper deduction — it also gives you peace of mind that you've done your part to leave the unit in good condition.</p>
<p>For a deeper look at how security deposit rules work statewide, the full text of <a href="https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billNavClient.xhtml?bill_id=202320240AB12" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">California AB 12</a> is publicly available.</p>

<p>Protecting your deposit ultimately comes down to two things: leaving the unit genuinely clean and having the documentation to prove it. Between packing, coordinating movers, and handling utility transfers, a professional <a href="/services/move-in-move-out-cleaning">move-in/move-out cleaning</a> service can take the deep-clean portion off your plate entirely, so you can focus on the rest of the move with confidence that the unit will pass inspection.</p>

<p>Getting ready to move? <a href="/contact">Request a free move-out cleaning quote from Spottly</a> before your final walkthrough.</p>
`,
