    excerpt:
      "Match cleaning frequency to household type and why biweekly suits most homes.",
    contentHtml: `
<p>Choosing how often to schedule house cleaning is one of those decisions that feels simple until you actually sit down and try to make it. Too frequent, and you're paying for a service your home doesn't need yet. Too infrequent, and you're constantly playing catch-up between visits, undoing whatever savings you thought you were getting. The right answer depends less on a general rule and more on how your household actually lives day to day — who's home, what's underfoot, and how much daily wear your rooms take on. Below is a practical breakdown of weekly, biweekly, and monthly cleaning, who each schedule tends to fit best, and the factors that should tip your decision one way or another.</p>

<h2>Weekly Cleaning</h2>
<p>Weekly service is the highest-frequency option most cleaning companies offer, and it's built for households where mess accumulates fast. If you have a large family, young kids who track in dirt and spill constantly, multiple pets shedding and tracking litter or mud, or a home that simply sees heavy daily traffic, a seven-day gap between cleanings is usually the sweet spot. It keeps kitchens and bathrooms consistently sanitized, floors free of the kind of buildup that turns into scuffing or staining, and dust from ever piling up enough to affect air quality.</p>
<p>Weekly cleaning also tends to suit people who entertain often or work from home and want their space looking presentable on a near-constant basis. Because the interval between visits is short, each appointment is also usually quicker and lighter — the crew is maintaining a baseline rather than resetting it, which is one reason this schedule pairs well with a <a href="/services/recurring-cleaning">recurring cleaning</a> plan rather than a series of one-off bookings.</p>

<h2>Biweekly Cleaning</h2>
<p>For most households, biweekly — once every two weeks — hits the best balance of upkeep and value. It's frequent enough to prevent the kind of grime buildup that makes cleaning harder and more time-consuming, but spaced out enough that you're not paying for attention a lightly-used guest bathroom or a rarely-touched dining room doesn't need yet.</p>
<p>This cadence works well for couples, small families, professionals who are out of the house most of the day, and anyone with a moderate amount of foot traffic and average pet or kid activity. Two weeks is also enough time for dust, soap scum, and general household residue to become noticeable without becoming stubborn, which means each visit can stick to a straightforward <a href="/services/standard-cleaning">standard cleaning</a> routine rather than requiring extra time to tackle buildup. It's a big reason biweekly is the most commonly chosen frequency for ongoing home cleaning — it matches how the average household actually gets dirty.</p>

<h2>Monthly Cleaning</h2>
<p>Monthly cleaning makes sense for smaller households, single occupants, or homes that see relatively light use — think a one- or two-person household without pets or young kids, or a second home that isn't lived in full-time. It can also work well for people who are diligent about daily tidying and light cleaning themselves but want a periodic deeper pass to handle the things that are easy to let slide, like baseboards, inside appliances, or window tracks.</p>
<p>The tradeoff with a monthly schedule is that four weeks is enough time for dust, soap scum, and grime to really settle in, especially in bathrooms and kitchens. Because of that gap, monthly appointments often benefit from being paired with, or occasionally upgraded to, a <a href="/services/deep-cleaning">deep cleaning</a> service rather than a standard visit — it gives the crew the time and scope needed to properly address a month's worth of buildup rather than just skimming the surface.</p>

<h2>Factors That Affect the Right Frequency</h2>
<p>Frequency isn't one-size-fits-all, and a handful of household-specific factors usually matter more than any general guideline. Here's what to weigh as you decide.</p>

<h3>Pets</h3>
<p>Shedding, dander, litter tracking, and muddy paws all add up quickly. Homes with one or more pets, especially dogs that go outside regularly, tend to need cleaning on the more frequent end of the spectrum to keep fur and dander from accumulating on floors, upholstery, and air vents.</p>

<h3>Kids</h3>
<p>Young children generate mess at a pace adults often underestimate — spilled snacks, sticky fingerprints, tracked-in dirt from school or the yard, and toys scattered through common areas. Households with kids, particularly younger ones, usually do better with weekly or biweekly service rather than monthly.</p>

<h3>Allergies</h3>
<p>If anyone in the household deals with allergies or asthma, more frequent cleaning helps keep dust, pet dander, and other airborne irritants from building up between visits. Bedrooms and living areas in particular benefit from regular attention when allergies are a concern, since these are the spaces where people spend the most time breathing indoor air.</p>

<h3>Entertaining</h3>
<p>If you regularly host guests, whether that's weekly game nights or occasional larger gatherings, your cleaning schedule should account for the extra wear those events create. Some households keep a steady biweekly plan and simply add a one-time visit before a big event, while frequent entertainers often find weekly service keeps things guest-ready without the scramble.</p>

<h3>Home Size</h3>
<p>Larger homes have more surface area to maintain, which means dust and dirt have more places to accumulate even with the same number of occupants. A spacious house with several bathrooms and living areas may need more frequent attention than a compact apartment housing the same size family, simply because there's more square footage collecting daily grime.</p>

<h2>Adjusting Your Schedule Over Time</h2>
<p>The frequency that makes sense for your home today won't necessarily be the right fit forever. Life changes — a new baby, a pet joining the family, kids growing up and moving out, a shift to working from home, or even just a change in how often you're hosting — all shift how quickly your home accumulates mess. It's worth revisiting your cleaning frequency every so often rather than treating it as a permanent decision.</p>
<p>A good approach is to start with the frequency that matches your current household type, then pay attention to how your home looks and feels in the days leading up to each visit. If rooms are looking noticeably dirty well before the next scheduled cleaning, that's a sign to increase frequency. If things still look mostly tidy right up until the crew arrives, you may be able to stretch the interval and save on cost without sacrificing cleanliness. Many households also find it useful to combine a steady recurring schedule with an occasional deeper visit around seasonal transitions or after particularly busy stretches, giving them the consistency of routine maintenance plus a periodic reset when needed.</p>

<p>Not sure which schedule fits your home? <a href="/contact">Request a free quote from Spottly</a> and we'll help you find the right rhythm.</p>
`,
